<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Timotheus (general)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 354 BCE</span></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Timotheus was an Athenian general and statesman of the fourth century BCE, son of the admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/conon">Conon</a>. His campaigns expanded the Second Athenian League; he captured <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samo">Samos</a> from a Persian garrison in 365 BCE after a ten-month siege and took several towns in the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aegean">Aegean</a>. Prosecuted after setbacks in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/social-war-357-355-bce">Social War</a>, he was heavily fined and died in exile at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcis">Chalcis</a> in 354 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/timotheus-general.jpg" alt="Timotheus (general)" width="320" height="176" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MYSIA,_Kyzikos._Early%E2%80%93mid_4th_centuries_BC._Portrait_of_Timotheos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
